~ start
Brian: Dan? Dan!!
Dan: Brian? Thank god. What's going on? Where did you all disappear?
Brian: I don't know; the last thing I remember is us four entering the house, and then all being alone in all this madness.
Dan: We need to find a way out, and fast. I feel like my mind is going crazy just from being here.
Brian: Agreed. Which way?
Dan: No idea. I have been walking this hallway for ages. It seems endless. The doors are all locked.
Dan: And they seem to shift colours, too.
Brian: Any strategy for getting out?
Dan: Well, you managed to enter, so some doors must be unlocked. We just have to find out which.
do SignalManager.fear.emit(1)
=> END

~ start1
Brian: It seems that just randomly trying doors will not get us anywhere.
Dan: Maybe, but what else can we do?
Brian: Let's think about it. What colour was the door you entered through?
Dan: Red! No, wait... yellow? Brown? Green? I can't recall...
Dan: It must be this place getting to me.
Brian: I entered through brown.
Brian: And from what I've seen, the doors in the house are also brown.
Brian: Let's try the brown doors and see if those work.
do SignalManager.fear.emit(2)

=> END

~ start2
Dan: No luck either.
Brian: Yeah.
Dan: Maybe we are looking at it wrong.
Dan: The doors change; if you walk back and forth, their colour is not what it was.
Dan: Maybe the real door does not change colour.
Dan: Maybe we should keep an eye out and see which stays the same.
do SignalManager.fear.emit(2)

=> END

~ start3
Brian: Well, still no way out.
Dan: I've tried so many brown doors, and all are locked.
Brian: Brown doors? I thought we were trying doors that do not change colour.
Dan: Wait, really? I thought brown ones.
Brian: I mean, you suggested this theory; I thought you knew we were testing it.
Dan: Did I? I am sorry. I thought... never mind, it's probably the stress getting to me.
Dan: I am starting to think we won't make it.
Brian: No, no, don't say that.
Brian: Think. Brown doors lead out. But doors here change colour...
Dan: What if we combine our theories?
Brian: You mean, a brown door that stays brown?
Dan: Yeah, I mean, those are quite rare; it's worth a shot.
Brian: True. And since some doors in this place are locked after entry, maybe we should try a few more.
Brian: If we give up after one, we might just find the locked one and miss the open one.
do SignalManager.fear.emit(3)

=> END

~ start4
Dan: I think we should stop trying.
Brian: Yeah, I know. I just don't want to give up yet.
Dan: Maybe there is something we are not seeing.
Dan: But I think it is more likely we are trapped.
Brian: I am sorry...
Dan: For what?
Brian: I was the one who suggested exploring this house.
Brian: And now all of you are in this madness.
Brian: Thanks to me.
Dan: Don't be.
Brian: Why not?
Dan: You had no idea what would happen.
Dan: And you are just as trapped as we are.
Dan: From where I am standing, we either both escape,
Dan: And then you have nothing to apologise to me for,
Dan: Or we both lose ourselves here,
Dan: And then I'd say we are equal.
Dan: You chose to explore this house.
Dan: We chose to explore it with you.
Dan: We both pay the price.
Dan: It's not like it's fair.
Dan: But that's life.
Brian: But if we get out, Margaret and Alice still might not...
Dan: You misunderstand me.
Dan: You do not escape blame by getting killed with us.
Dan: You do not deserve it either way.
Dan: You are a good person, Brian.
Dan: You are not responsible for the horrors of this place. I am sure of that.
Brian: (Maybe he is right.)
Brian: (I do share their fate.)
Brian: (I couldn't have known what this place was.)
Brian: (And it seems to torment me just like them.)
Brian: Thank you.
Brian: You are right, we make it out together, or die together.
Brian: But I won't leave you.
Brian: Now, what do we do about the door situation?
Dan: I think we exhausted all the door options.
Dan: The only thing left is to walk the hall.
Dan: Perhaps it is not infinite, just really long.
Dan: Or perhaps we will find something to aid us.
Brian: Ok, we walk.
Brian: Just stay close, don't get lost, ok?
Dan: Right behind you.
do SignalManager.fear.emit(-5)

=> END

~ cycle
Dan: Need a break?
- yes
	Dan: Ok, we can rest for a while.
	Dan: It's not like there is any immediate danger.
	Dan: And we have been walking for quite some time.
	Brian: (How long have we been walking for?)
	Brian: (Minutes? Hours? Days?)
	Brian: (Best not to dwell on that.)
	do SignalManager.fear.emit(6)
- no
	Dan: Then let's keep walking.

=> END